# A-Net Online at 2400, and the call that produced --max-evm.  The banner
# arrives whole and then the far end hangs up: the level falls to nothing
# between 21 and 22 seconds and the receiver went on framing its own
# decisions all the way down, which is where the second, corrupted copy
# of the banner in the reference comes from.
#
# So this fixture pins two things at once -- that the banner decodes, and
# that the junk behind it stays the length it is now.  See
# docs/robustness.md; 140 bytes became 60 when the decision-error gate
# went in, and it should not go back up.  It came down again, from 143
# bytes of decode to 124, when the 2400 bit/s gain learned to follow a
# step in level (Modec.QAM, qrAgcStep); the banner itself is unchanged.
#
# Re-minted when the V.22bis receiver's gain stopped chasing its own
# data (see Modec.V22): the banner is identical and the expect: line
# above still holds, but the far end hangs up part way through repeating
# it and what the receiver makes of the silence afterwards is different
# rubbish from the rubbish it made before.  Fourteen bytes more of it.
#
# Re-minted again when the calling side stopped being told to make
# sixteen-way decisions for one block at the moment circuit 112 went ON
# (see Modec.Handshake.rxRateFor).  This fixture is the only one of the
# twenty that noticed, and what it noticed was not the fix: the first
# 107 bytes -- the whole banner, and the second copy up to where the far
# end hangs up -- are identical, and the six bytes are all in the tail.
#
# Which is worth writing down, because the tail is what this fixture
# pins.  It is chaotic, and now measured: adding noise 60 to 100 dB
# below the signal -- below this recording's own quantisation floor, and
# far smaller than any code change -- moves the byte count over a range
# of fifteen, on the code that produced the previous reference.  So a
# tolerance of 0 here is pinning one draw from that distribution.  Keep
# it, because it is an alarm worth having and a change worth looking at
# every time; but a byte count that moves is a reason to go and read the
# decode, not a verdict on its own.
source:    recordings/20260906T004535-a-net-online-v22bis.wav
seconds:   22
role:      originate
modes:     v22bis,v22
v8:        no
connect:   V22bis 2400
expect:    Synchronet External POTS Support v1.32-Win32 Copyright 2020 Ro
tolerance: 0
